  "query_preview": "..\n\nA longwave trough resides along the Pacific Coast late this \nmorning, anchored around an upper low spinning off the WA/OR \ncoastline near 130W. A shortwave trough is noted ejecting through \nthe lower Colorado River Valley, and is pulling an anomalously \nmoist airmass into the Desert Southwest/southern Great Basin. \nMeanwhile an Atmospheric River (AR) is noted along the base of the\nlongwave trough near 20N. This initial shortwave passage today, \nfollowed by the main upper trough late Saturday night through \nSunday, will bring 2 rounds of heavy precipitation across southern\nand eastern Utah. The first round this afternoon/evening will be \nmore convective in nature, while the second round late Saturday \nnight into Sunday associated with the inland-penetrating AR will \nbe predominantly stratiform. See the Hydrology section below for \nmore details on the heavy rain and flood potential through Sunday.\n\n\nAs the initial shortwave ejects northward, ongoing convection \nacross southwest Utah into northern AZ will lift into central and\neventually northern Utah later this afternoon through this \nevening. In the wake of this feature, lingering moisture will\nmaintain a chance of showers and isolated thunderstorms Saturday,\nhowever with the forecast area remaining between today's ejecting\nshortwave and the approaching longwave trough axis, large scale \nforcing will remain limited which will keep convective coverage\nisolated to perhaps scattered in nature.\n\nAs the upper low off the Pacific NW coastline begins its eastward\nprogression late Saturday, the aforementioned AR will spread\ninland around the southern end of the Sierra, and into the\nsouthern Great Basin/Desert Southwest Saturday night, reaching\nsouthern Utah by early Sunday. The approaching upper low will\ntranslate through the Great Basin Sunday through Sunday night,\nwith the combination of the two bringing widespread and\npotentially heavy precipitation across the forecast area Sunday \nthrough Sunday night. Colder air in the wake of the surface-700mb\ntrough axis will spread into the area late Sunday through Sunday\nnight, with snow levels falling from 9000+ ft Sunday afternoon to\nbelow 7000 ft by early Monday. This will result in accumulating\nhigh elevation snowfall across the forecast area Sunday night\nthrough Monday morning. Assuming the post-frontal environment\nevolves as currently forecast, a favorable orographic setup \nSunday night into Monday morning looks likely across the upper \nCottonwoods and other northern/central mountains favored in NW \nflow, where snow totals in excess of 12\" are currently forecast. \n\nIn the wake of this system, mid level ridging will bring a cool\nand stable airmass into the area through mid-week, followed by\nanother series of troughs beginning Thursday. Considerable\nuncertainty exists regarding how this pattern evolves beyond\nWednesday, thus confidence is low regarding the probability,\ntiming and coverage of precipitation later next week.",
